Cache hit ratio comes out to 0% since there are zero reads, so the caching here is a net loss of about half a dollar — small money, but worth noting. Looking at Terra's daily write pattern across its six active days, writes consistently run around 65% of input tokens, roughly 900 write tokens per invocation, suggesting it's writing fresh cache every single request without ever reading from it — likely due to differing prompt prefixes or requests spaced beyond the 30-minute cache TTL, possibly compounded by cross-region routing. For Sonnet 5, with 46.4M input tokens across 8,299 invocations and no cache metrics available at all, there's a clear missed caching opportunity averaging over 5,600 tokens per invocation, well above the 1,024 token minimum — though without confirmed pricing I can't properly estimate the hypothetical savings from caching this traffic.

